    "text": "The dispatch office was a bunker of light and sound, a small, square room carved out of the night. Inside, the air was thick with the smell of burnt coffee and the low, constant hum of the control board. Green and amber lights blinked on the panel, tracing the slow, deliberate progress of freight trains across the state. Each one was a blip, a number, a voice crackling over the radio.\n\nRaymond had been a dispatcher for thirty-two years. The night shift was his. It was a quiet, solitary kingdom, and he was its keeper. He knew the rhythm of the rails better than his own heartbeat. The 11:45 to Chicago, the 1:20 coal drag from the Powder River Basin, the 3:05 auto-rack from Detroit. They were his nocturnal companions.\n\nAround two in the morning, a lull settled over the airwaves. The trains were all in their slots, moving through the darkness like blood through veins. Raymond leaned back in his worn leather chair, the springs groaning in protest. He reached for the bookshelf behind him, his fingers brushing past dog-eared safety manuals and regulatory binders. He was looking for something to occupy the quiet. His hand landed on a thin, cloth-bound volume, its spine faded to a pale grey. It was a timetable from the Burlington Northern line, dated 1978. A relic.\n\nHe opened it, the paper brittle and smelling of dust and old glue. The familiar, dense columns of numbers and station names were a comfort, a map of a",
